      "math_academy_id": "q-6676",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem",
      "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x+12?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $12$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }12}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(2)$ is not a factor of $3.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
