    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-14142",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a",
      "knowledge_point": "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial",
      "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
