    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-39038",
      "knowledge_point_id": "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1",
      "knowledge_point": "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem",
      "problem": "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+18x-18$ has a root in the interval $(2,7),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(2,7),$ are $3$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$3$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$18$\n$-18$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$3$\n$-12$\n$18$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-4$\n$6$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-4x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}-4x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(6) \\\\  & =16-24 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$",
